findings.message:
In the newly added url_match_auth_nego() the final block gates on the NTLM flags, not the Negotiate flags (copy/paste from url_match_auth_ntlm()):

  if(m->want_ntlm_http || m->want_proxy_ntlm_http) {      /* lib/url.c:1226 */
    m->found = conn;
    if((m->want_nego_http && conn->http_negotiate_state != GSS_AUTHNONE) ||
       (m->want_proxy_nego_http && conn->proxy_negotiate_state != GSS_AUTHNONE)) {
      m->force_reuse = TRUE;
      return TRUE;
    }
    return FALSE; /* get another */
  }
  return TRUE;

This condition can never be true at this point: url_match_conn() only reaches url_match_auth_nego() when url_match_auth_ntlm() returned TRUE without force_reuse, and (with USE_NTLM) that only happens when both want_ntlm_http and want_proxy_ntlm_http are FALSE; without USE_NTLM those bits are never set at all (they are only assigned under #ifdef USE_NTLM in url_attach_existing(), lib/url.c:1356-1366). So in every build the block is unreachable and the function degenerates to 'return TRUE' for every Negotiate-wanting transfer whose credentials match.

Consequences:
1. m->force_reuse is never set for Negotiate, so the comment's requirement 'We MUST use a connection where it has already been fully negotiated' is not enforced. A transfer that wants Negotiate can be matched to a connection that is only partway through a SPNEGO handshake (http_negotiate_state == GSS_AUTHSENT / GSS_AUTHRECV) — url_match_auth_nego() returns TRUE, url_match_conn() falls through to url_match_multiplex_limits() and sets m->found = conn (line 1305). Injecting a second transfer's request into a connection that is mid-handshake is precisely the situation the analogous NTLM code deliberately avoids, and it lets one transfer's request be sent on a connection whose authentication state was established by another transfer's exchange.
2. Conversely, an already fully-authenticated Negotiate connection is no longer forcibly preferred, so curl can silently open a fresh unauthenticated connection while an authenticated one exists, defeating the fix's intent.

The follow-up check 'else if(m->force_reuse) return TRUE;' at lib/url.c:1292 is therefore also dead code.

